stack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fa048cda6-76d1-4878-8d67-fbc8e38dbc93_1080x1350.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><div class=\"image-link-expand\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset\"><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" class=\"icon-noB79L\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2 icon-noB79L\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p class=\"button-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://vigeoretreats.com/retreat/the-wholehearted-woman-retreat/&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Learn More about this Retreat&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:&quot;button-wrapper&quot;}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary button-wrapper\" href=\"https://vigeoretreats.com/retreat/the-wholehearted-woman-retreat/\"><span>Learn More about this Retreat</span></a></p><div><hr></div><h3><strong>New to Retreats with Erin?</strong></h3><p>Here’s what to expect.</p><p class=\"button-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://mindfulmoments.sg/retreats/what-to-expect&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Retreat Guide&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:&quot;button-wrapper&quot;}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary button-wrapper\" href=\"https://mindfulmoments.sg/retreats/what-to-expect\"><span>Retreat Guide</span></a></p><p></p>",
  "body_text": "Five sessions into our Dhammapada series, we arrive at the theme of happiness. The teachings are so often heard as suffering, suffering, suffering… and yet there really is a whole chapter on happiness.\n\nAh, so happily we live, without ambition among those with ambition.\n\n(verse 199, trans. Gil Fronsdal)\n\nNow don’t be mistaken — this is not about withdrawing from the world. We are part of this ecosystem, and we are participating. \n\nWe simply stop letting its agitations reproduce inside us. \n\nSomeone else’s anger in the office does not have to become our anger. \n\nA consumer culture repeating “more, more, more” does not have to turn into a sense of lacking within us.\n\nThe text tells us that victory gives birth to hate, that the defeated sleep in anguish, and that those who give up both sleep happily. \n\nModern life is very much organised around a scoreboard — who performed better, whose opinion won, who was right. \n\nThere is suffering on both sides of that board, and this includes the winning side, when we hold tightly to what we win.\n\nThere is nothing wrong with winning, of course, and nothing wrong with aspiration; in fact, our practice carries one of the largest aspirations of all — the complete freedom from suffering. \n\nThe invitation is a little quieter: \n\nCan I give myself wholeheartedly to something without making winning the condition for my peace?\n\nWith this, our understanding of happiness moves from a merely pleasurable experience to freedom from what we are bound to or compelled by.\n\nHappiness comes from what we choose to put down, not what we acquire or add to our lives.\n\nSo this week, before reaching for one more thing, let us notice what is already present, and see that just being here, alive and breathing, is perhaps enough.\n\n---\n\nAt Take A Pause Live on Insight Timer over 22-23 August 2026, we cultivated a sense of enough-ness with a body awareness meditation. We then listened to the poem, So Much Happiness, by Naomi Shihab Nye. \n\nMeditation Practice\n\nTap Play to Practice Together:\n\nThis post is free for some time, and then archived for paid subscribers to support a sustainable, long-term sharing of mindful content. To support Erin’s work, upgrade to receive lifetime access to the full archive and ongoing updates.\n\nShare\n\n---\n\nThe Wholehearted Woman Retreat\n\n1–5 October 2026 · Siem Reap, Cambodia\n\nwith Erin Lee\n\n*Early bird rate available\n\nLearn More about this Retreat\n\n---\n\nNew to Retreats with Erin?\n\nHere’s what to expect.\n\nRetreat Guide",
